when Apple announced that you can now  get an 8 core processor and the new 2019  macbook pro the first thought that came  into everyone's mind is that it's  definitely gonna thermal throttle if the  six core macbook pro did so we're gonna  find out if that's true by running our  classic Cinebench stress test on both  the new MacBook Pro with the 2.3  gigahertz eight core processor  32 gigs of RAM and Vega 20 graphics as  well as on our similarly configured in  price 2018 model with the 2.6 gigahertz  six core i7 processor the reason it's  not the six core i9 processor is because  we saw literally zero gains in video  editing compared to the i7 and we expect  to see the same results with a new eight  core hence why we didn't pay an extra  two hundred dollars for the faster eight  core processor what we're gonna do is  run cinnamon jar 25 times back-to-back  to ensure that the system heats up and  the fans stabilize the temps shortly  after starting our first run the CPU  boosts up to four point three nine  gigahertz nowhere near the advertised  4.8 gigahertz turbo boost speed which  isn't a surprise because that number is  rated off just one core out of eight  running by itself with temps under 50  degrees Celsius the clock speed then  lowers down to around three point three  gigahertz just before the temps reach 99  degrees as soon as this happens the  wattage drops to sixty the temps  instantly shoot down to 90 degrees in  the clock speed is now sticking to  around 3.0 gigahertz the temp slowly  starts picking up again and stays at  around 97 degrees while the clock speed  hovers around 2.9 gigahertz and so we  finished the first run with a score of  3096  before instantly re running the test  again just for comparison the $5000 base  iMac pro scores around 3700 points in  this test starting the second run the  CPU spikes to a lower 4.3 one gigahertz  before coming back down to the same 2.9  gigahertz clock speed range  unlike the first run the fans are now at  full blast so it's able to keep the  clock speed higher at around 3.0  gigahertz at the same 97 degrees  tempering  now finishing with an even higher 3160  once core starting the third run the CPU  spikes up to only 3.6 5 gigahertz  showing the effect temps  have on turbo boost speeds now here we  notice something very interesting the  clock speed is able to stay at around  three gigahertz while the temps are  sitting at or lower 94 to 95 degrees but  out of nowhere the CPU clocks down to  2.9 gigahertz even though it doesn't  need to  since the temps aren't too high in fact  they go as low as 91 degrees with plenty  of thermal Headroom for faster clock  speeds but it doesn't ever go above 3.0  gigahertz almost like it's being  intentionally limited the third run  finishes with a 3125 score the fourth  run spikes up to 3.7 gigahertz turbo  boost so it seems like the system's  thermals have stabilized we can now see  that the temperature stays at 93 degrees  while at 2.9 gigahertz and 95 degrees  while at 3.0 gigahertz now completely  stabilized the fourth run scored three  thousand 154 points in the fifth scored  three thousand one hundred forty-seven  making for a five run average score of  three thousand one hundred and thirty  six now before we draw some conclusions  and thoughts from this let's quickly  talk about the results from the 2018  MacBook Pro basically every single run  hit around 4.03 to 4.04 gigahertz Peak  turbo boost before coming down to around  three point one to three point three  gigahertz before finally stabilizing at  around three point four to three point  oh eight gigahertz attempts ranging from  around 96 to 97 degrees and  interestingly we didn't see temps  randomly going down to 91 or 92 degrees  like we did on the 2019 Pro each run was  also very consistent and scored exactly  2425 points every time comparing both of  them we see that the 2019 model hit a  much higher four point three nine  gigahertz turbo boost  but the stabilized clock speed was lower  at around 2.9 to 3.0 gigahertz compared  to 3.0 four to 3.0 8 gigahertz  however the stabilized temps were lower  staying between 93 and 95 degrees  compared to 96 and 97 degrees this shows  us that Apple decided to keep the temps  a bit lower on the new model instead of  slightly turning up the power to hit  around 97 degrees I read through some  forums and some users mentioned that the  high  of previous MacBook Pros could have been  wearing down the components in the  butterfly keyboard so if that's true it  would make sense why Apple would want  attempts to stay lower and it could  actually fix the keyboard issues once  and for all the best part is that even  though temps were lower than they could  have been we still got an average score  that's 30% higher than the 2018 MacBook  Pro so we get more power with cooler  temps at the same exact price snazzy  labs tested the new a core MacBook Pro  and he found that Apple seems to have  set a full load clock speed target which  instead of turning up the clock speed  whenever there's extra thermal Headroom  the machine would slow down the fans for  a quieter experience he also replaced  the thermal paste with liquid metal and  didn't find much of a difference stating  that the 2019 MacBook Pro features much  better thermal paste than previous  MacBook Pros this definitely shows in  our testing as the 2019 macbook pro  idled at only 35 degrees Celsius  compared to 45 degrees with that 2018  model now that's a huge difference we  also tested Cinebench R 20 using a  single thread to see the difference in  performance and we saw that the 2019 Pro  hit a maximum of 4 point 5 gigahertz  turbo boost compared to four point one  seven on the 2018 pro and we scored four  hundred and sixty-six points compared to  four hundred and twenty two so not only  do we get two extra cores but each core  is faster as well so let's answer our  original question the 2019 eight core  macbook pro obviously thermal throttles  right no it actually fixes the  throttling issues from before performing  much better while at lower temps which  may possibly even fix the keyboard  issues at the same time if you're  thankful that we ran these tests and  you're thinking of buying a 2019 MacBook  Pro for yourself smash that like button  and use the links in the description  below to help support this channel make  sure yourself by clicking that circle  above and check out one of those two  videos right there thanks for watching  and we'll see you in the next video  [Music]
 

